---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

export const title = "Park Butte Fire Lookout";
export const date = "2026-08-28";
export const excerpt =
  "Spending the night in a historic fire lookout in the North Cascades, one of the most memorable nights of the year.";
export const tags = ["hiking", "camping", "north-cascades", "washington"];
---

<SiteLayout title={`${title} — Saachi K Gupta`}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p style="opacity:0.7; font-size: 13px;">{date}</p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Park Butte Lookout sits above the meadows on the shoulder of Mt. Baker, close enough that the mountain stops being scenery and starts being something you're standing next to.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The lookout itself dates to 1932, built in the wave of fire detection towers that went up across the Cascades after the catastrophic fires of 1910 convinced the Forest Service that spotting smoke early was worth the investment. Most of those towers came down once aerial and satellite detection took over. Park Butte's is one of the few left intact in the range, kept standing today by Skagit Alpine Club volunteers. It's first-come-first-served, no reservation, which is part of why a weekday felt like the move.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Sleeping in a fire lookout had been on my Washington bucket list for a while, and Monday, June 15th, was the day to finally do it: probably my last unemployed weekday in Seattle, and a first-come-first-served lookout is easiest to claim on a weekday. I built a detailed itinerary in my Notes app and felt genuinely prepared. (More on the days leading up to this one <a href="/reflections/final-weekend-of-funemployment-in-seattle">here</a>.)
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I'd been anxious about packing all morning, but by nine I was through my whole pre-trip checklist and ready. Lunch with Owen that day, plus finally locking in my plan, with an 8pm turnaround if the lookout felt off, made me feel brave enough to actually go. So did having the route mapped on <a href="https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/washington/park-butte-lookout-trail">AllTrails Pro</a> (7.5 miles round trip, 2,200 feet of gain, by their numbers) with the <a href="https://www.wta.org/go-hiking/hikes/park-butte">WTA page</a> pulled up too. I left Seattle around 1:45, with a 3:45 ETA to the trailhead, drove up Baker Lake Road, turned onto seven miles of gravel, and parked around 4:10pm.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Up to the lookout</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The hike up was about four miles and 2,300 feet of climbing, just under two hours of moving time, crossing Sulphur Creek and Schriebers Meadow before climbing through old-growth forest into Morovitz Meadow. The junction with Railroad Grade, the alternate route toward Easton Glacier on Baker, was the part of the trail I'd never seen before, and it was stunning. There were a few creek crossings and a snow section that felt doable but sketchy. I should have brought microspikes, or at least poles.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I made it to the lookout at 7pm. It was just me. Gorgeous, and quiet in the specific way a place gets when you know you're the only person in it for the night. I ate snacks, journaled by hand on the porch, and just soaked in the view, watching the light change over the peaks. By 8:12 it was still light out and I was already pooped.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    At 8:30, three teenagers showed up to make vlogs, which broke the idyllic solitude I'd been so proud of. It turned out to be a good thing. Sleeping up there completely alone, with the wind working on the shutters all night, would have been scary without them. I fell asleep soon after.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Sunrise, then down</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I was up before dawn the next morning for the sunrise. The teenagers had cell service somehow, so I FaceTimed my parents before I hiked out, then started down at 6am to make it back to the car by 8. Four miles downhill, including two of snow, then a two-hour drive, a noon call, a 1:30 call, and a flight that evening: it felt impossible to say in one sentence, but I told myself I had time and could stay calm. The snow on the way down was sketchy in a few spots, but I made it to the car right on time, at 8, the same four miles in reverse, another two hours of moving time.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    My Strava caption for that one says it best: "2 hrs lookout to car but 10++++ car to home." I wasn't exaggerating enough.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The pothole that wasn't</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I started driving out at 8:11. At 8:23, two miles down the same gravel road I'd come in on, something slammed underneath the car and the tire blew. I told a friend it was a pothole, because that's what it felt like. I had no cell signal out there, only satellite texting, so I sent what I could piece together: the tire had popped, something else had broken, I had no idea what. Then I waited. Four hours, alone on a forest road in the North Cascades, watching the morning's tight schedule come apart hour by hour.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">The tow</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The tow truck picked me up at 3pm for a 98-mile haul from the trailhead to Autologic in Bellevue, dragging the whole way on the left rear. I'd already missed the noon and 1:30 calls. When a friend asked if I might still make the 6:30 flight, I told her I wasn't making it, I was still way up the gravel road with the tow. I dropped the car at Autologic that evening. I was physically fine, great even. The car was not.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">What actually happened</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    In January, I'd had the rear brakes serviced and hadn't thought about it since. I'd only put about 2,000 miles on the car between then and June. At the time, I assumed it was on me: driving too boldly on a potholed gravel road, hitting something wrong. It wasn't until days later, on calls with the shop, that I found out how much more serious it actually was. The left rear brake caliper had come off the car entirely while I was driving through the mountains, and it was the caliper, dangling and dragging, that punctured the tire. The bolts from January's service hadn't been tightened. Straight negligence, nothing I'd done. The repair came to over $1,000, and the shop that did the original work agreed to cover it.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">A little of the evening back</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I still didn't have real cell service by late afternoon, so a friend called the shop for me to sort out the drop-off. It turned out nobody was in a rush. The shop was fine with me leaving the key if I got there after close, which meant I still had a night ahead of me instead of losing it entirely. So I went anyway to Duck Dodge, the informal Tuesday-night sailboat race on Lake Union, one last one before three weeks away.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I took the light rail home after, ate takeout, showered, and put on compression boots, the same recovery routine I use after a long race. I decided I wasn't fixing the car before I left. No rush. It would happen. The next morning I got on a plane for a three-week family holiday, the kind of trip that comes around often enough that leaving still makes me a little emotional every time. I wanted to arrive healthy and in one piece. That, and the lookout, and Duck Dodge. Whatever the day had cost me, I still had those.
  </p>

